# -------- go + gate tools (ADR-0018 + ADR-0020) --------
GO_VERSION=1.27.0                                                      # latest LTS as of 2026-08-24
GO_SHA256_AMD64=675c26c449cbb18fc24b74650de1eabbae6e16f64326fd85a283fb3b58280685  # computed-at-pin: 2026-08-24
GO_SHA256_ARM64=51798d2c42d0e1c6ed7fd9f48728b4193abac9e8aad6dbac2fe96a81f5909bda  # computed-at-pin: 2026-08-24
GOFUMPT_VERSION=0.11.0                                                 # latest LTS as of 2026-08-17
GOLANGCI_LINT_VERSION=2.13.1                                           # latest LTS as of 2026-08-24
# GOVULNCHECK 1.1.4 COULD NOT READ go1.27 SOURCE, and that is why this row moved
# 6 releases in 1 edit. govulncheck vendors `golang.org/x/tools/go/ssa` to build
# the call graph it reports reachability from, and 1.1.4 vendors x/tools v0.29.0,
# whose SSA builder predates go1.27 syntax: it panics `unexpected expr:
# *ast.KeyValueExpr` at builder.go:958 and exits 2. Measured in
# `ghcr.io/gophersys/base:latest` (go1.27.0) against gophersys/libs
# `go/secrets` — 1.1.4 panicked, 1.7.0 (x/tools v0.49.0) answered
# "No vulnerabilities found" at exit 0 on the same tree. It is not a flake and no
# flag avoids it: a scanner that cannot parse the language the repository is
# written in reports 0 findings for the reason that it read 0 code.
#
# THE PIN LOOKED MAINTAINED WHILE IT WAS FROZEN, and that half is ledger work of
# its own rather than a fact about this tool. `_build/upstreams.txt` gives this
# pin `github-release|golang/vuln`, and that row RESOLVES — so no weekly run ever
# went red about it. golang/vuln stopped cutting GitHub Releases at v1.1.4
# (2025-01-13) and went on TAGGING through v1.7.0 (2026-08-13), so
# `releases/latest` answers 1.1.4 forever and every Monday reported "no move".
# The datasource is wrong rather than broken: govulncheck is installed with
# `go install golang.org/x/vuln/cmd/govulncheck@v${PIN}`, so its real upstream is
# the go module proxy and not a release page. THE ROW MOVES TO `no-autobump` IN
# THIS CHANGE, because leaving it would make the weekly UNDO this bump: with the
# pin at 1.7.0 the resolver still answers 1.1.4, and `collect_bumps` reads that
# difference as a mover. Teaching the resolver to read the proxy is the real fix
# and it is a change of its own.
GOVULNCHECK_VERSION=1.7.0                                              # latest release as of 2026-08-25; 1.1.4 could not parse go1.27 — see the note above
GOSEC_VERSION=2.28.0                                                   # latest LTS as of 2026-08-17
HNSLINT_VERSION=0.1.0                                                  # latest as of 2026-08-11 - gophersys/hnslint
GREMLINS_VERSION=0.6.0                                                 # latest LTS as of 2026-06-13 - needs >=0.6.0 for unleash --exclude-files
BENCHSTAT_REF=latest                                                   # x/perf is untagged; track @latest
# sqlc generates eden's persistence layer and its `verify` verb re-generates and
# diffs, so an absent sqlc is a DRIFT CHECK THAT NEVER RAN. eden
# `apps/platformgateway/persistence/ctl.sh:29` calls `require_cmd sqlc`, and
# measured 2026-08-26 that binary was in no image this repository publishes
# (`grep -rin sqlc` over this tree matched nothing) — so the verb exited 127 in
# every lane since it was written and no gate in either repository could say so.
# Blueprint P0-4.
SQLC_VERSION=1.31.1                                                    # latest stable as of 2026-08-26
DELVE_VERSION=1.27.1                                                   # latest stable as of 2026-08-16

# -------- parsing + QA --------
# A digest sits beside its version, in this same file, so a bump is the version
# row plus one row per platform THE DOWNLOAD'S OWN CASE ARMS name — 3 adjacent
# lines for the dual-arch pins below, and 2 for a _NOARCH one. The count comes
# from the arms as written and never from the sanctioned set: a download with one
# arm has one row and is complete, not incomplete. 2 homes apart means the bump
# misses one, and a stale digest FAILS the build at the download naming the pin.
#
# The weekly bump moves every one of those rows together, and `bump_pin` REFUSES
# a call that names fewer than the homes declare. It wrote the version and 1
# digest row until 2026-08-18, so every _ARM64 row went stale the moment its pin
# moved.
YQ_VERSION=4.53.6                                                      # latest LTS as of 2026-08-24
YQ_SHA256_AMD64=c5f056448f973ae7d39b5401949648a78f2dc1947d6a8eb65be60d5c504b9385  # computed-at-pin: 2026-08-24
YQ_SHA256_ARM64=88a1016bc1d657375a35864e4f44b6f333df8ff97b559f51bba0adcb2169df09  # computed-at-pin: 2026-08-24
HADOLINT_VERSION=2.15.1                                                # latest stable as of 2026-08-18
HADOLINT_SHA256_AMD64=c7187db94eeeeca956519a6af171adc31453941a1e777961f6e680f697c8c507  # computed-at-pin: 2026-08-18
HADOLINT_SHA256_ARM64=f6198ef8090f404dbb771abfee086eb8c48ac177f30da7fd3510aca35b344b5d  # computed-at-pin: 2026-08-17

# -------- THE GATE TOOLS (ledger #117) --------
# 2 rows of this file also govern `ctl.sh validate`, and they are the 2 tools
# whose VERSION decides a verdict about the repository's own content:
# SHELLCHECK_VERSION and HADOLINT_VERSION. `validate` refuses to judge with any
# other version, so a developer's run and a CI run give the same answer or no
# answer. Raising either one is a version change like any other, and it may turn
# new findings red.
#
# It was 1 for a long time, and the missing half cost a green nobody could
# trust. `resolve_image_platforms` in _ctl/lib.sh passed `ctl.sh validate` on a
# mac and failed it in CI with SC2119 + SC2120 (run 32111944450): the verb used
# whatever shellcheck was on PATH, and 2 machines held 2 versions of it. The gate
# now names the tool, the version it found, the version this file pins, and the
# remedy.
#
# THE EXEMPTIONS, and why each one is not a pin. `validate` reaches for 5 tools;
# the other 3 take no row here, and the reason is the SHAPE of the question the
# verb asks them:
#
#   jq       parses project.json and every devcontainer.json. The questions are
#            "does this parse" and "what string is at .image" — answered by the
#            DOCUMENT, not by the parser's release. A jq that disagreed about
#            either would be a broken jq, not a stricter one.
#   yq       answers images.yaml through manifest_yq. Same shape, and it already
#            holds the line that matters: 4.x on PATH, or the YQ_VERSION pin
#            through docker, because 3.x is a different tool wearing the name.
#   docker   the container route of the 2 linters. It transports a verdict and
#            never renders one; the version that decides is the image tag, and
#            that tag is the pin.
#
# The exemptions are DECLARED in ctl.sh (GATE_TOOL_EXEMPT) rather than only
# written here, and _ctl/tests/toolchain-parity.test.sh holds the 2 tables to
# set equality with the tools the verb reaches for. A tool that is neither
# pinned nor exempt is the silence the rule exists to break.
#
# The shell linter comes from the ubuntu archive and not from a release download, so
# this row takes the apt shape ZSH_VERSION takes: the Dockerfiles install
# `shellcheck=${SHELLCHECK_VERSION}*`, which is what makes the pin a property of
# the image rather than a claim about it. The archive has published exactly 1
# version of it for noble, so there is nothing to choose between.
SHELLCHECK_VERSION=0.9.0                                               # the only binary the ubuntu noble archive publishes (0.9.0-1) as of 2026-08-19
KUBECONFORM_VERSION=0.8.0                                              # latest release as of 2026-08-10
KUBECONFORM_SHA256_AMD64=9bc2bffbf71f261128533edaf912153948b7ff238f9a531ae6d34466ec287883  # upstream-published: https://github.com/yannh/kubeconform/releases/download/v0.8.0/CHECKSUMS
KUBECONFORM_SHA256_ARM64=1f53fc8e81258197a35e8603054162a5af1de8c5af13746c71ab680d9534ed87  # upstream-published: https://github.com/yannh/kubeconform/releases/download/v0.8.0/CHECKSUMS
GITLEAKS_VERSION=8.30.1                                                # latest LTS as of 2026-06-13
GITLEAKS_SHA256_AMD64=551f6fc83ea457d62a0d98237cbad105af8d557003051f41f3e7ca7b3f2470eb  # upstream-published: https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v8.30.1/gitleaks_8.30.1_checksums.txt
GITLEAKS_SHA256_ARM64=e4a487ee7ccd7d3a7f7ec08657610aa3606637dab924210b3aee62570fb4b080  # upstream-published: https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v8.30.1/gitleaks_8.30.1_checksums.txt

