# -------- base only: what base installs and cloud does not --------
# The cloud reduction dropped Rust, ansible and oci-cli (image-architecture
# §2.2), so these 4 rows feed 1 image. A pin of 1 image still belongs here: a
# second home is what this change removed, not a second image.
#
# RUST_CHANNEL is a channel and never a version — rustup takes `stable`, and
# resolving it to a number would freeze the channel the pin exists to follow
# (_build/upstreams.txt says the same, as its no-autobump reason).
RUST_CHANNEL=stable                                                    # latest LTS as of 2026-04-19
OCI_CLI_VERSION=3.90.3                                                 # latest LTS as of 2026-08-24
ANSIBLE_VERSION=14.3.1                                                 # latest LTS as of 2026-08-17
ANSIBLE_CORE_VERSION=2.21.3                                            # latest LTS as of 2026-08-17

# -------- ready components, consumed by NO image today (extension points) ----
# 2 rows, and this section held 5 until base joined this file. oci-cli and the 2
# ansible rows moved to the section above: base has installed all 3 since long
# before its pins arrived here, so "consumed by NO image" was true of the cloud
# family and of nothing else.
TERRAFORM_VERSION=1.14.8                                               # latest LTS as of 2026-04-19
AWS_CLI_VERSION=2.34.32                                                # latest LTS as of 2026-04-20

# -------- hardware only: the KiCad ECAD toolchain --------
# 5 rows that feed `hardware` alone. It is a CHILD of cloud and it still reads
# THIS file: images.yaml gives it `pins: versions.env`, so its ARGs are
# value-less like cloud's and no 4th PIN_VALUE_HOME is minted. A pin of 1 image
# belongs here for the reason the base-only section gives — a second home is
# what the collapse removed, not a second image.
#
# KICAD_PPA_VERSION IS NOT A VERSION. It is the major line inside a PPA NAME:
# `ppa:kicad/kicad-${KICAD_PPA_VERSION}.0-releases`. The archive behind that
# name publishes 10.0.5~ubuntu24.04.1 today, and this row would still read 10.
# Same class as PYTHON_PACKAGE, and _build/upstreams.txt says so as its
# no-autobump reason.
#
# THE PPA PUBLISHES noble arm64, and that is why this image declares no
# `platforms` narrowing. Read from the Launchpad API on 2026-08-18,
# https://api.launchpad.net/1.0/~kicad/+archive/ubuntu/kicad-10.0-releases
# ?ws.op=getPublishedBinaries&binary_name=kicad&exact_match=true&status=Published
# answers `10.0.5~ubuntu24.04.1` Published for BOTH ubuntu/noble/amd64 and
# ubuntu/noble/arm64, date_published 2026-07-24. The same query for
# kicad-symbols, kicad-footprints and kicad-packages3d answers the same version
# Published on noble arm64 as well. So all 4 packages exist on both sanctioned
# architectures, and an arm64 build fails on a missing package only if the PPA
# stops publishing one — which apt reports by name.
# RUFF_VERSION DIVERGES FROM THE CONSUMER ON PURPOSE, and this is the only place
# that says so. research-hardware ci/Dockerfile pins ruff 0.16.0 — "matches the
# working local .venv exactly". This row takes 0.16.3, the latest stable on pypi,
# because the rule of this repository is to select the latest LTS or stable
# release for a new tool, and the weekly bump would move the pin there on the
# first Monday anyway. The other 3 python pins need no such note: 1.4.8, 1.0.2
# and 9.1.1 are BOTH the consumer's pins and the latest on pypi.
#
# THE COST IS NAMED. ruff changes lint verdicts between point releases, the way
# hadolint does. A rule added in 0.16.1, 0.16.2 or 0.16.3 can turn
# research-hardware's suite red on the day it repoints at this image. That red is
# EXPECTED, it is a finding about the consumer's code and not about this pin, and
# it gets read on the consumer's own pull request. Do not answer it by lowering
# this row without Mateo's decision — 2 pins for 1 tool is the drift the one pin
# home exists to end.
KICAD_PPA_VERSION=10                                                   # the KiCad major line inside the PPA name, as of 2026-08-18
KIUTILS_VERSION=1.4.8                                                  # latest stable on pypi as of 2026-08-18, and the consumer's pin
SEXPDATA_VERSION=1.0.2                                                 # latest stable on pypi as of 2026-08-18, and the consumer's pin
PYTEST_VERSION=9.1.1                                                   # latest stable on pypi as of 2026-08-18, and the consumer's pin
RUFF_VERSION=0.16.4                                                    # latest stable on pypi as of 2026-08-24; the consumer pins 0.16.0 — see the note above

# -------- ui only: the headless browser --------
# 1 row that feeds `ui` alone. Same shape as the hardware section above: a CHILD
# of cloud with `pins: versions.env`, so its ARGs are value-less and this stays
# the one home.
#
# CHROME_MAJOR_VERSION IS NOT THE VERSION APT INSTALLS, and it is not spelled in
# ui/Dockerfile either. google-chrome-stable is installed UNPINNED — the
# base/cloud shape for an apt repo somebody else moves, which is how
# docker-ce-cli is installed — because Google's stable component publishes
# exactly ONE version of the package at a time. A `=${major}.*` constraint would
# therefore not hold an older browser: it would fail the BUILD with
# "Version '152.*' for 'google-chrome-stable' was not found" about 4 weeks after
# it was written, and a build that cannot run is a worse reporter than a smoke
# that goes red. THE 4-WEEK ESTIMATE WAS MEASURED: this text was written against
# major 151 on 2026-08-18 and google moved the stable component to 152 by
# 2026-08-26.
#
# SO THIS ROW IS ASSERTED AND NOT CONSUMED, AND IT IS ASSERTED ON ONE
# ARCHITECTURE. `.ci/smoke.sh` compares it against `${DENSUI_CHROME} --version`
# with the `prefix` reader — the shape KICAD_PPA_VERSION uses for a major line
# inside a name — inside the container the gate build LOADED, and that container
# is `SMOKE_PLATFORM`, `linux/amd64`. So the day Google moves the major, the
# amd64 smoke goes red NAMING this pin and the publish stops. The answer is 1
# edit here, after a human reads the release. `_build/upstreams.txt` carries the
# matching no-autobump row and its reason.
#
# THE ARM64 BROWSER IS VERSION-UNVERIFIED, and this row does not claim otherwise.
# The publish step builds the arm64 leg on the mini from the same Dockerfile, and
# an UNPINNED apt install re-resolves `google-chrome-stable` at that moment — a
# different minute, and potentially a different major, from the amd64 leg the
# smoke just read. NOTHING compares the arm64 browser to this row. That is not a
# hole this image opened: it is the standing gap the sanctioned-platform section
# of `.claude/rules/00-identity.md` records — "the arm64 content of every image
# is NOT smoke-gated at publish time" — and its recorded follow-up, smoking arm64
# out of the registry after the push, is what would close it here. This image is
# the first one where the gap has a MOVING input behind it rather than the same
# pinned tarball both legs fetch, so it is the loudest case for that follow-up.
#
# Read on 2026-08-26, Google's own repo metadata says the stable component holds
# 152.0.7977.64-1 for amd64 AND arm64 — the same version on both, which is why
# this image declares no `platforms` narrowing and why research-ui's open
# question Q2 (version skew across arches) does not arise TODAY. The first build
# of this image said the same thing from the other side, one major earlier: in
# rehearsal run 32151769443 the amd64 leg fetched `google-chrome-stable amd64
# 151.0.7922.137-1` and the arm64 leg `google-chrome-stable arm64
# 151.0.7922.137-1`, out of 2 independent apt resolutions that installed 98 and
# 96 packages. Each is a reading of one afternoon and not a guarantee: the 2
# components move on their own, and only the amd64 one is ever read by an
# assertion. The measurement is beside the images.yaml entry.
#
# THE 151 → 152 MOVE IS WHAT THIS ROW EXISTS TO CATCH, and it caught it. Google
# published 152.0.7977.64-1 to the stable component, the unpinned apt install
# took it, and the amd64 smoke went red naming this pin — `FAIL: version drift:
# reports 152.0.7977.64, and CHROME_MAJOR_VERSION` — in run 32905565450 and in 3
# of the 4 runs before it. NO BUMP MACHINERY MOVED THE BROWSER: this row is
# `no-autobump` in `_build/upstreams.txt` on purpose, so the weekly never touches
# it, and the browser moved because the apt install is deliberately unpinned. The
# red IS the design working. The remedy is the one the upstreams row names — a
# human reads the release and edits this line — and that is what this change is.
#
# THIS DOES NOT FIX #97, the densui wait_pre flake, and nothing here claims to.
# What a pinned-and-asserted browser major buys is the ability to DIAGNOSE it ON
# THE AMD64 VARIANT: a flake whose browser version no run records cannot be tied
# to a browser version, and until this row every image that ran those gates
# carried whatever Chrome the build day happened to publish. On the arm64 variant
# the diagnostic is NOT available, because no run records that browser's version
# at all — so an arm64 reproduction of #97 still cannot be tied to a version, and
# a reader must not read this row as evidence about it.
CHROME_MAJOR_VERSION=152                                               # the google-chrome-stable major the AMD64 smoke asserts; google's stable apt repo as of 2026-08-26 (152.0.7977.64-1, both arches)
